-- `create or modify persistent entity` on an EXISTING entity deleted every
-- access rule on it. ako/mxcli-rest FINDINGS, "create or modify persistent
-- entity silently deletes every access rule".
--
--   create or modify persistent entity App.Order ( Ref: String(20) );
--   exec -> Modified entity: App.Order          <- the whole output
--   mxcli check -> passed
--   describe entity App.Order -> not one `grant` left
--
-- MEASURED on a real project (ako/CapTrackV3), both engines, mxbuild 11.14.0.
-- Re-running one domain-model script over eight entities:
--
--   access rules    174 -> 132
--   member entries  1352 -> 1071
--   mx check        0 errors -> 288 errors, every one CE2729
--
-- THE 288 IS A CORRECTION TO THE REPORT, which said `mx check` stays clean. It
-- does — on an entity nothing is bound to yet. The loss is silent EXACTLY where
-- nothing depends on the access, and announces itself only once pages exist:
--
--   [CE2729] No read access to attribute 'Name' in entity 'CapTrack.Department'
--            for user role 'TeamLead'   at Text 'mdName'
--
-- IDEMPOTENCE DOES NOT HELP. A byte-identical re-run strips the rules just as
-- completely: the rebuilt document genuinely differs from the stored one, so it
-- is not elided (ADR-0008). Re-running a domain-model script is the ordinary way
-- to work, which is what made this reachable.
--
-- THE CAUSE WAS STRUCTURAL, not a missing case. The modify branch built a fresh
-- entity from the AST and swapped it in, so every field MDL has no words for went
-- with it. Documentation and indexes had each been added back to a carry list
-- after their own defect report; access rules would have been the third, and the
-- external-source and OData fields the next.
--
-- Dropping an attribute the statement omits and rebuilding the ones it names are
-- INDEPENDENT: an entity can be modified in place. So the fix inverts the
-- direction — start from what is stored, overwrite only what the statement
-- declares — and the carry list has no tail. A reflection test now fails on any
-- Entity field that is in neither group.
--
-- ONE THING IS STILL REMOVED BY OMISSION, and deliberately: EXTENDS. There is no
-- "extends nothing" spelling, so preserving it would make an inheritance
-- impossible to remove. It is now REPORTED like a dropped attribute, which is the
-- only part of this that was silent before.
--
-- A SECOND DEFECT FELL OUT OF THE FIRST, and it is the more interesting one. A
-- fix that starts PRESERVING something reaches states no prior code could
-- produce. When the rewrite drops EVERY attribute a rule covered, the prune
-- empties that rule's member list — and the LEGACY engine's
-- ReconcileMemberAccesses skipped any rule whose list held only the storage
-- marker, so the entity's NEW attributes never got an entry. CE0066 again, this
-- time from the opposite direction. Measured on the real BusinessEvents 3.12.0
-- module: the Administrator rule went 5 members -> 0 on legacy and 5 -> 1 on
-- modelsdk, from the same script.
--
-- That engine split is the whole diagnosis. Identical input, 0 errors on one
-- engine and CE0066 on the other, localises the defect to the legacy path in one
-- measurement and makes the codec engine the reference for what the document
-- should contain. The guard is now `len(maArr) == 0` — no storage marker at all
-- — because a rule with no entries on an entity that HAS members is exactly the
-- out-of-date state CE0066 names.
--
-- Verify:
--
--   mxcli exec  entity-modify-preserves-access-rules.mdl -p app.mpr
--   mxcli exec  entity-modify-preserves-access-rules.mdl -p app.mpr   -- again
--   mxcli -p app.mpr -c "describe entity AccP.Order"
--     -- both grants still there, both times
--   mx check -p app.mpr        -- 0 errors
--
-- Use `mx check` DIRECTLY, not `mxcli docker check`: that command runs
-- `mx update-widgets` first and can mask a model-level failure.
--
-- Then perturb:
--
--   stub mergeDeclaredOntoStoredEntity to return the rebuilt entity
--     -> every `grant` above disappears, `Modified entity:` is still the only
--        output, and mxcli check still passes. At scale: 174 -> 132 and 288
--        CE2729 errors.
--
--   drop `extends System.FileDocument` from AccP.Export below
--     -> reported as a dropped member. Before: silent.
--
--   restore the `len(maArr) <= 1` early break in ReconcileMemberAccesses, then
--   run AccP.Wiped below on --engine legacy
--     -> CE0066 on the module. The rule survives with an empty member list and
--        Kept never gets an entry. Unaffected on modelsdk, which is how the
--        defect was localised.

create module AccP;
/

create or modify module role AccP.Reader;
/

create or modify persistent entity AccP.Order (
  Ref: String(20),
  Amount: Decimal default 0,
  Note: String(100)
);
/

grant AccP.Reader on AccP.Order (create, delete, read (Ref, Amount, Note), write (Ref, Amount));
/

-- THE REPORTED CASE. Restating the entity must leave the grant above untouched.
-- Run this file twice: the second run is the byte-identical re-run that the
-- report expected to be safe and was not.
create or modify persistent entity AccP.Order (
  Ref: String(20),
  Amount: Decimal default 0,
  Note: String(100)
);
/

-- THE CONTROL THAT COST A DAY. An entity's access rules also govern its
-- INHERITED members, and those never appear in its own Attributes list. The
-- obvious predicate for pruning — "not among the rebuilt entity's attributes" —
-- therefore empties every rule on a specialisation that owns no attributes, and
-- mxbuild reports CE0066 "Entity access is out of date". The first version of
-- this fix did exactly that, and only an entity shaped like this one exposes it.
create or modify persistent entity AccP.Export extends System.FileDocument (
);
/

grant AccP.Reader on AccP.Export (create, delete, read (Name, Contents), write (Name, Contents));
/

create or modify persistent entity AccP.Export extends System.FileDocument (
);
/

-- CONTROL: the other half of the contract is unchanged. An attribute the
-- statement omits IS removed, warned about, and its member entry goes with it —
-- a preserved rule naming an attribute that no longer exists would dangle.
create or modify persistent entity AccP.Order (
  Ref: String(20),
  Amount: Decimal default 0
);
/

-- THE SECOND DEFECT, in its smallest form: a rewrite that drops EVERY attribute
-- the rule covered and introduces a new one. The prune empties the member list,
-- and the reconcile then has to fill it — which the legacy engine refused to do
-- for an empty list, leaving Kept uncovered and the module at CE0066. Run this
-- under both engines; they must agree.
create or modify persistent entity AccP.Wiped (
  Gone: String(20),
  AlsoGone: Integer
);
/

-- `create, delete` is not decoration: without an entity-level right, a rule whose
-- every member right is None grants nothing at all, and Mendix says so — CE0004
-- "No access rights granted". That is Mendix being right about a rule that has
-- become pointless, not a defect, but it would mask the one under test.
grant AccP.Reader on AccP.Wiped (create, delete, read (Gone, AlsoGone), write (Gone));
/

create or modify persistent entity AccP.Wiped (
  Kept: String(20)
);
/
